{"candidate":{"claim":"Extend Pass 1 with a support relation S and define the acceptance set as the least fixed point of the operator Φ(X) = G_att(X ∪ S[X]), where G_att(Y) is the standard grounded extension of the attack graph restricted to Y. This preserves determinism and polynomial cost when S is acyclic, and reinstatement remains a derived property of the underlying attack graph.","counterconditions":["Monotonicity of Φ depends on acyclicity of S; if S has cycles, the iteration may oscillate and no unique fixed point is guaranteed.","The composition G_att(X ∪ S[X]) may not correspond to any single Dung argumentation framework, so reinstatement may not be a derived property of the original attack graph.","Polynomial cost may be lost if S[X] is expensive to compute or if the grounded extension must be recomputed from scratch at each iteration, though naive iteration is still O(n^2 * cost(G_att)).","The support relation S is not currently part of the spec; adding it requires a new input and may break compatibility with existing committed roots.","The acyclicity condition on S is a strong restriction; real support relations may be cyclic, and the proposal does not handle cycles.","The operator Φ may not be monotone if S is not acyclic, so the least fixed point may not exist or may not be unique.","Reinstatement: the proposal must show that if an argument is accepted, all its attackers are defeated; this may fail because support can reintroduce defeated arguments.","The validity of every committed root must be preserved; support edges could accidentally validate a root that was previously invalid."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"0c302d11abcd","quote":"The grounded extension itself was never the problem: given the edges, the whole question lives in the construction of the edges."},{"block":"0378275f","quote":"Carriage is a relation, not a field."}],"mechanism":"Define a two-relation argumentation framework (A, attack, support). Compute the least fixed point of Φ(X) = G_att(X ∪ S[X]) where S[X] = {b | ∃a∈X: (a,b)∈support}. This is a monotone operator on the complete lattice of subsets of A if support is acyclic, so the least fixed point exists and is unique. The iteration is polynomial: each step computes a grounded extension on a subgraph, which is polynomial, and the number of steps is bounded by |A|.","sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_001"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}}